{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 20:43:45 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-23 03:43:45 +07 (Indochina Time)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0cad7-2f30-7b82-ae67-288922310f75\n\n# Hash-agile DaoJing and yin.vm plan\n\n## Decision\n\nDaoJing will use BLAKE3-256 as its default hash algorithm while retaining SHA-256 permanently for existing content and explicitly frozen contracts.\n\nAddresses must describe both:\n\n1. the canonical encoding profile whose bytes were hashed; and\n2. the hash algorithm applied to those bytes.\n\nThe new canonical form is:\n\n```text\n:segment/<encoding-id>+<algorithm-id>-<lowercase-hex-digest>\n```\n\nThe first new default address is therefore:\n\n```text\n:segment/print-v1+blake3-<64 lowercase hex>\n```\n\nThe existing form remains valid permanently:\n\n```text\n:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>\n```\n\nIt is parsed as the legacy canonical spelling of:\n\n```clojure\n{:encoding :jing.print/v1\n :algorithm :sha256\n :digest    \"...\"}\n```\n\nFor that exact profile/algorithm pair, `segment-key` continues to emit the legacy spelling rather than introducing a second canonical address for the same identity.\n\nBLAKE3 is pinned to its standard unkeyed 256-bit output. Variable-length BLAKE3 output, keyed hashing, and derivation-key modes are different algorithms for registry purposes and are not admitted under the `blake3` identifier.\n\n### Why the encoding identifier is necessary\n\nThe proposed `:segment/<algo>-<hex>` generalization is sufficient only while there is one canonical encoder. It cannot preserve old content after the documented print-to-CBOR migration: verification of an old address would otherwise re-encode its payload with the new default encoder and reject it.\n\nThis repository already promises both an encoder migration and continued validity of old content. An algorithm-only address cannot satisfy both promises.\n\nThe proposed form is therefore CID-like rather than merely multihash-like: it carries a representation profile and a hash algorithm while remaining readable EDN. It does not adopt IPFS’s binary CID or multihash encodings.\n\nFuture CBOR addresses can use forms such as:\n\n```text\n:segment/cbor-v1+blake3-<digest>\n:segment/cbor-v1+sha256-<digest>\n```\n\nNaming the future profile does not implement or settle CBOR in this epic.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- An address is derived solely from canonical bytes, their encoding profile, and their hash algorithm.\n- Address validation always uses the profile and algorithm carried by the address, never the current defaults.\n- Defaults affect minting only. They do not affect parsing, reading, or verification of an existing address.\n- Registry membership is closed and immutable. There is no runtime registration or hidden mutable state.\n- Unknown algorithms, unknown encoding profiles, malformed lengths, uppercase hex, and non-hex digests fail closed.\n- SHA-256 remains supported for as long as a SHA-256 address or frozen SHA-based VM contract can exist.\n- The digest is 32 bytes for both initial algorithms. Digest length nevertheless belongs to the registry rather than being hardcoded into the parser.\n- A bare digest is not a durable content identifier. Persisted identities must carry their algorithm and encoding profile.\n- `segment-address?` recognizes every supported profile/algorithm form, including legacy SHA-256 addresses.\n- All new default DaoJing materialization uses BLAKE3 after the default-flip phase.\n\n## DaoJing design\n\n### Closed registries\n\n`dao.jing` owns two small immutable registries.\n\nThe algorithm registry initially contains:\n\n```clojure\n:sha256 {:address-id \"sha256\", :digest-bytes 32, ...}\n:blake3 {:address-id \"blake3\", :digest-bytes 32, ...}\n```\n\nThe encoding registry initially contains the current transitional encoder:\n\n```clojure\n:jing.print/v1 {:address-id \"print-v1\", ...}\n```\n\nThe legacy address parser maps bare `sha256-...` to `:jing.print/v1` plus `:sha256`.\n\nRegistry descriptors may refer to private host-specific digest functions, but the registries are immutable code, not runtime extension points. Adding an algorithm or encoding profile requires a reviewed source change and conformance fixtures.\n\nDefaults are explicit immutable values:\n\n```clojure\ndefault-hash-algorithm\ndefault-content-encoding\n```\n\nThey begin as `:sha256` and `:jing.print/v1` during compatibility work. The hash default changes to `:blake3` only after all validators have become address-directed.\n\n### Public primitive surface\n\nAdd algorithm-neutral primitives:\n\n```clojure\n(digest-bytes algorithm bytes) ; lowercase hex\n(digest-string algorithm s)    ; UTF-8, then digest-bytes\n```\n\nUnknown algorithms throw with the requested identifier.\n\nKeep these permanently as explicit compatibility and contract functions:\n\n```clojure\n(sha256 s)\n(sha256-bytes bytes)\n```\n\nAdd equivalent explicit BLAKE3 functions if useful for known-answer tests:\n\n```clojure\n(blake3 s)\n(blake3-bytes bytes)\n```\n\nThese names never follow the default.\n\n### `canonical-bytes`\n\nCurrent one-argument behavior remains:\n\n```clojure\n(canonical-bytes value)\n```\n\nAdd an explicit profile form, preferably through a final options map:\n\n```clojure\n(canonical-bytes value {:encoding :jing.print/v1})\n```\n\nDuring this epic the only implemented encoding remains the existing order-normalized printer. The profile seam exists so address verification does not later depend on an ambient default.\n\nWhen CBOR lands, it adds a new profile rather than changing what `:jing.print/v1` means.\n\n### `content-hash`\n\nRetain the current one-argument call:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash value)\n```\n\nIt hashes the bytes from the current default encoding with the current default algorithm.\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash value {:encoding profile\n                     :algorithm algorithm})\n```\n\nImplementation must be `canonical-bytes` followed by `digest-bytes`; there must be only one byte stream and one digest operation.\n\nBecause its result remains bare hex, `content-hash` is suitable for local calculations and explicitly versioned/frozen contracts, but not for newly persisted standalone identity fields. New durable identity should use `segment-key` or another self-describing envelope.\n\n### `segment-key`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-key value)\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-key value {:encoding profile\n                    :algorithm algorithm})\n```\n\nThe one-argument form uses the defaults.\n\nFormatting rules:\n\n- `:jing.print/v1` plus `:sha256` emits the existing `:segment/sha256-...` form.\n- Other supported combinations emit `:segment/<encoding-id>+<algorithm-id>-<digest>`.\n- The namespace must be exactly `segment`.\n- Names and digest text are lowercase ASCII.\n- Output must survive `pr-str` and EDN reading unchanged.\n\n### Address parsing\n\nAdd one authoritative parser:\n\n```clojure\n(parse-segment-address address)\n;; => {:encoding :jing.print/v1\n;;     :algorithm :blake3\n;;     :digest \"...\"\n;;     :legacy? false}\n;; or nil\n```\n\nAll other address accessors delegate to it:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-address? address)\n(segment-encoding address)\n(segment-algorithm address)\n(segment-hash address)\n```\n\n`segment-hash`, `segment-encoding`, and `segment-algorithm` throw on invalid input. `segment-address?` remains a total predicate.\n\nParsing must match registered address identifiers, not accept arbitrary strings that merely resemble an algorithm name.\n\n### Address-directed verification\n\nAdd one public verification operation:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-matches? address payload)\n```\n\nIt:\n\n1. parses the address;\n2. selects the carried encoding and algorithm;\n3. canonically encodes the payload under that profile;\n4. hashes those bytes;\n5. compares the resulting digest to the carried digest.\n\nIt returns false for malformed or unsupported addresses. An optional throwing companion is not necessary unless it materially simplifies backend diagnostics.\n\nThis function becomes the sole normal way outside `dao.jing` to validate an address/payload pair.\n\n### `materialize!`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload)\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload opts)\n```\n\nThe default form mints with the current defaults. The explicit form passes its encoding and algorithm to `segment-key`.\n\nOn `:present`, verify the stored value with `segment-matches? address stored`; do not compare `content-hash` under the current default with `segment-hash`.\n\nThis explicit arity permits tests, repair tools, and controlled publication of legacy SHA content without changing global defaults.\n\n### `get`\n\nThe signature remains:\n\n```clojure\n(get handle address not-found)\n```\n\nIt accepts every address recognized by `segment-address?`, including legacy SHA-256 and new BLAKE3 forms. It still rejects arbitrary keys before consulting the backend.\n\n`get` need not rehash every successful read; backend insertion/replay checks and higher-level verified readers retain their current responsibilities. Where a consumer already verifies fetched content, it must use `segment-matches?`.\n\n### Internal SHA implementation\n\nThe hand-written ClojureDart SHA-256 implementation remains because old addresses and frozen contracts require it. It must not be removed after BLAKE3 becomes the default.\n\n## Findings from the call-site audit\n\nThe claim that all hashing is confined to `dao.jing` is false.\n\n### Direct `jing/sha256` consumers\n\nThe current tree contains these direct consumers:\n\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/descriptor-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/image-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/node-hash`\n- `dao.jing.dht/node-id`\n\nThese are explicit non-default SHA contracts and should remain so.\n\nThe task named `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` and register contract tests, but those files do not exist in this checkout. The register format exists as design in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`, where R is explicitly specified as SHA-256. The plan therefore governs that implementation when it lands rather than pretending there is current source to edit.\n\n### Hardcoded SHA label outside DaoJing\n\n`yin.vm/primitive-profile` constructs:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k.pp/sha256-<jing/content-hash description>\n```\n\nThis is a real correctness bug after the default flips: it would label a BLAKE3 digest as SHA-256. Change this call to request the legacy print-v1/SHA-256 profile explicitly. Do not silently change the primitive-profile contract in this epic.\n\n### Bare persisted content hash\n\n`dao.space.index/checkpoint-candidate` persists `:schema-hash` as a bare `jing/content-hash`, and `restore` recomputes it using the ambient default. This is not algorithm-agile.\n\nNew candidates should carry:\n\n```clojure\n:schema-address (jing/segment-key schema)\n```\n\n`restore` verifies it with `segment-matches?`.\n\nFor existing candidates carrying a 64-character `:schema-hash`, verification explicitly treats it as the historical print-v1/SHA-256 digest. No bulk candidate rewrite is required. New candidates do not emit the old field.\n\n### Existing-address validation sites\n\nThese sites currently recompute with the default and must use `segment-matches?`:\n\n- `dao.jing/materialize!`\n- `dao.jing.mem/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.file/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.file/validate-codec-round-trip!`, using the address’s profile\n- `dao.jing.remote/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.remote.step` verification of a `:present` response\n- `dao.jing.dht/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.dht` fetched-content verification\n- `dao.jing.dht.node` store request validation\n- `dao.data.btree.storage` optional hydrated-blob verification\n- `dao.space.index/read-manifest`\n- `yin.vm.content` row and vector fetch verification\n- `yin.vm.macro` row-address verification\n- `yin.vm.semantic` claimed segment verification\n- `yin.vm.completion` reconstructed-image address verification\n- `yin.vm.ledger` row, output, and record verification\n- `yin.vm` semantic-bytecode row validation\n\nGeneration-only calls to `segment-key` do not require source changes; after the flip they intentionally mint the new default.\n\nDocstrings and errors in `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.file`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.jing.dht.kad`, `dao.space.index`, and `dao.jing.stream` that say “SHA-256 only” must be generalized.\n\nThe DHT may continue routing by the 256-bit digest alone. Both initial algorithms produce 64 hex characters, and the full segment address remains the storage key. DHT node IDs remain explicitly SHA-256; changing that keyspace is unrelated to content-address agility.\n\n## yin.vm H and R disposition\n\nH and R must remain SHA-256 for this epic.\n\nFor the implemented stack format:\n\n- keep `descriptor-hash` and `image-hash` on explicit SHA-256;\n- keep descriptor data declaring `:hash :sha256`;\n- do not repin the golden H corpus;\n- add a regression proving a DaoJing default change does not change H.\n\nThe older `yin.vm.debruijn` dimension and node hashes likewise remain explicit SHA-256. They are format identities, not DaoJing segment addresses.\n\nFor the planned register format:\n\n- implement `register-descriptor-hash` and `register-hash` with explicit SHA-256;\n- retain the documented R formula and descriptor declaration;\n- do not couple R to `default-hash-algorithm`.\n\nH or R should change only through their own contract-version process. If another format change already requires a version bump and full golden re-pin, switching that format to BLAKE3 may be considered in that separate review. It must never happen merely because DaoJing’s storage default changed.\n\nJing addresses for stored stack or register images will use the new DaoJing default independently. H/R remain indexes over executable-format bytes; a Jing segment address remains the identity of the stored Jing value. These identities must not be conflated.\n\n## Host libraries\n\nUse the portability spike’s validated choices:\n\n- JVM: `io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3` 1.3\n- ClojureScript/Node: `@noble/hashes` 2.4.0\n- ClojureDart: `blake3_dart` 1.0.0\n\nPin exact versions. Do not use floating ranges for a content-identity dependency.\n\n### Maintenance risks\n\nThe JVM dependency is pure bytecode and MIT licensed but stale, with its last Maven release dating to 2020. Acceptance requires:\n\n- source and license review before adoption;\n- official BLAKE3 known-answer tests at chunk and tree boundaries;\n- a cross-host fixture corpus;\n- isolation behind `digest-bytes`;\n- a documented replacement procedure demonstrating byte identity before changing providers.\n\nThe Dart dependency is pure Dart and passed official vectors, but has only one release and a short history. It receives the same fixture and replacement discipline. It is also the Dart host’s first external cryptographic dependency, so dependency resolution and release-build tests are explicit gates.\n\n`@noble/hashes` is actively maintained, but its ESM/package integration with the current Shadow-CLJS build must be exercised in both test and optimized release builds.\n\nProvider replacement does not change addresses when the replacement implements the same pinned BLAKE3-256 algorithm. A provider update that changes any conformance digest is rejected.\n\n## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\n\nThe hash-agility work should land before, and independently of, the CBOR migration.\n\nThe required order is:\n\n1. introduce profile-aware parsing, explicit algorithms, and address-directed verification while SHA-256 remains the default;\n2. prove mixed legacy SHA and new BLAKE3 operation;\n3. flip the hash default to BLAKE3 under `:jing.print/v1`;\n4. land canonical CBOR later as a new encoding profile and change `default-content-encoding` in its own reviewed migration.\n\nThis deliberately permits two generations of new addresses—print-v1/BLAKE3 and later CBOR-v1/BLAKE3—because both remain independently verifiable. It avoids combining a cryptographic-provider change with a canonical-byte change in one diagnosis surface.\n\nIf the owner elects to coordinate the two defaults operationally to reduce duplicate materialization, phases 1 and 2 must still land first. The BLAKE3 and CBOR default flips may then share a release, but their registries, fixtures, and failure attribution must remain separate.\n\nThe hash epic does not implement CBOR or decide its canonical rules. It only prevents that already-planned migration from making earlier addresses unverifiable.\n\n## Phased rollout\n\n### H0: contract and audit\n\n    New: address grammar, registry descriptors, cross-host fixtures\n    Existing edits: tests and design documentation only\n    Must not change: current SHA addresses, current default, VM H/R values\n\nComplete when:\n\n- the legacy and expanded address grammars are pinned;\n- official BLAKE3-256 fixtures and representative canonical-value fixtures are recorded;\n- every source call to the five current Jing hash/address entry points and every `sha256` literal is classified as minting, validating, or a frozen non-Jing contract;\n- the missing register implementation is recorded as future integration rather than current code;\n- malformed, unknown-algorithm, wrong-length, uppercase, and EDN round-trip cases are specified.\n\n### H1: host primitives and closed registries\n\n    Dependencies: the three pinned BLAKE3 providers\n    Existing edits: dao.jing, dependency manifests, focused primitive tests\n    Default: SHA-256 plus print-v1\n    Must not change: output of existing one-argument segment-key\n\nComplete when:\n\n- `digest-bytes` and `digest-string` support SHA-256 and BLAKE3-256 on all three hosts;\n- official and cross-host vectors are byte-identical;\n- explicit BLAKE3 segment keys use `print-v1+blake3`;\n- existing SHA segment keys remain byte-for-byte unchanged;\n- parser/accessor tests pass for both forms;\n- unknown registry identifiers fail closed;\n- CLJ, optimized CLJS, and CLJD builds resolve and execute their providers.\n\n### H2: mixed-address storage\n\n    Existing edits: DaoJing backends and every validation call site listed above\n    Default: still SHA-256\n    Must not change: VM H/R, DHT node IDs, primitive-profile identities\n\nComplete when:\n\n- every validator derives encoding and algorithm from the claimed address;\n- one store can hold the same payload under legacy SHA and BLAKE3 addresses;\n- memory, file replay, remote, stepped remote, DHT, B-tree hydration, DaoSpace manifests, and yin.vm content loading accept and verify both;\n- tampering is rejected under both algorithms;\n- an old SHA file store reopens without rewrite;\n- a fetched old SHA object verifies while the test temporarily binds or injects a BLAKE3 minting default;\n- the checkpoint schema identity is self-describing for new candidates and explicitly SHA-verified for legacy candidates.\n\n### H3: BLAKE3 default\n\n    Existing edits: default declaration, default-address expectations and fixtures\n    New default: :jing.print/v1 plus :blake3\n    Must not change: explicit SHA contracts or stored legacy content\n\nComplete when:\n\n- one-argument `content-hash`, `segment-key`, and `materialize!` select BLAKE3;\n- all newly minted default segment addresses use `:segment/print-v1+blake3-...`;\n- explicit SHA minting still produces the legacy spelling;\n- all three host suites agree on default addresses for the shared corpus;\n- old SHA addresses remain readable and verifiable through every backend;\n- stack H, de Bruijn dimension/node hashes, DHT node IDs, and primitive-profile identifiers retain their pinned values;\n- no register golden values are changed merely by this phase.\n\n### H4: portability and maintenance hardening\n\n    Existing edits: CI, dependency notes, conformance resources\n    Must not change: address grammar or digest output\n\nComplete when:\n\n- boundary vectors cover empty input, non-ASCII UTF-8, 1023/1024/1025-byte inputs, multi-chunk inputs, and canonical payload fixtures;\n- randomized cross-host corpora compare complete addresses, not merely digests;\n- dependency versions and licenses are recorded;\n- provider-replacement instructions require the complete corpus before acceptance;\n- optimized CLJS and release CLJD builds are included rather than relying only on development modes;\n- error data identifies algorithm and encoding profile without including whole sensitive payloads.\n\n### H5: documentation closure\n\n    Existing edits: dao.jing and directly affected yin.vm/DaoSpace design docs\n    Must not implement: CBOR itself\n\nComplete when:\n\n- `dao.jing.md` distinguishes encoding profile, hash algorithm, digest, and segment address;\n- all SHA-only statements are corrected;\n- the open CBOR item says it introduces a new encoding profile rather than silently changing the meaning of old addresses;\n- the H/R documents state that executable-format hashes are frozen independently of the DaoJing default;\n- the legacy-address and legacy-checkpoint policies are explicit;\n- source docstrings agree with the implemented grammar and defaults.\n\n## Test obligations\n\nThe minimum acceptance matrix is:\n\n- SHA-256 and BLAKE3 known-answer tests on CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD.\n- Identical `digest-string` behavior for non-ASCII UTF-8.\n- Identical canonical bytes and full addresses across hosts.\n- EDN print/read round-trip for both address forms.\n- Strict rejection of invalid namespace, profile, algorithm, case, length, and characters.\n- Same payload under two algorithms produces two valid, independently retrievable entries.\n- Default changes do not affect verification of existing addresses.\n- Backend collision and `:present` read-back paths use the address-carried profile.\n- File replay handles mixed historical and new records.\n- Remote and DHT peers can exchange both address forms.\n- Existing hardcoded SHA fixture stores remain usable without rewriting.\n- VM H and de Bruijn node-hash goldens do not change.\n- When register code lands, R goldens likewise remain independent of DaoJing defaults.\n- A simulated second encoding profile proves that verification dispatches by the address rather than the ambient encoding default before real CBOR is enabled.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\nThis plan does not:\n\n- rehash, rewrite, rename, or delete existing SHA-256 content;\n- make every old object acquire a BLAKE3 alias;\n- adopt IPFS binary multihash or CID serialization;\n- introduce runtime algorithm registration or algorithm negotiation;\n- implement canonical CBOR or alter its value-domain decisions;\n- change DHT node identities or Kademlia distance rules;\n- change yin.vm H, R, de Bruijn node hashes, or their contract versions;\n- use BLAKE3 keyed mode, derivation mode, or variable-length output;\n- remove SHA-256 implementations or dependencies;\n- redesign DaoJing’s synchronous handle, effect stream, retention, or garbage collection;\n- treat hashes as security signatures or add authenticity/provenance semantics.\n\nOld data is retained under its original identity. No bulk migration is required. New defaults affect new minting, consistent with immutable content-addressed storage and the project’s preference not to build compatibility machinery that rewrites established data.\n\n## Divergences from the baseline\n\n1. **The new address includes an encoding profile.**  \n   I reject `:segment/<algo>-<hex>` as insufficient because the documented CBOR migration would make old payloads unverifiable under a changed ambient encoder. The proposed `:segment/<encoding>+<algo>-<hex>` form preserves both old and future content identities.\n\n2. **Call-site changes are not close to zero.**  \n   Hash generation is mostly centralized, but address verification is duplicated across backends, DHT transport, B-tree storage, DaoSpace, and yin.vm. Every one of those comparisons currently uses the default hash and must become address-directed.\n\n3. **The centralization claim is factually false.**  \n   `yin.vm.debruijn_code`, `yin.vm.debruijn`, and `dao.jing.dht` call `jing/sha256` directly. `yin.vm/primitive-profile` also hardcodes a `sha256-` label around `jing/content-hash`.\n\n4. **The primitive-profile call must change.**  \n   It would otherwise emit a SHA-labeled BLAKE3 digest after the default flip. Its contract remains SHA-256, but the call becomes explicit.\n\n5. **DaoSpace’s bare checkpoint `:schema-hash` requires migration.**  \n   It is persisted without an algorithm tag and is recomputed using the default. New candidates must carry a self-describing schema address, while old candidates are interpreted explicitly as historical SHA-256.\n\n6. **The hash rollout need not wait for CBOR.**  \n   An encoding-aware address permits BLAKE3 to land safely on the current bytes. CBOR can then arrive independently as a new profile. Combining both default flips remains an operational option, not an architectural dependency.\n\n7. **The register implementation named by the task is absent.**  \n   Only its design document exists in this checkout. The plan freezes its documented SHA-256 R contract when implemented rather than claiming current source or golden tests can be edited.\n\n8. **“Old data stays valid forever” is made enforceable rather than assumed.**  \n   Keeping SHA in an algorithm registry alone is insufficient. Validators must select both the algorithm and encoder from each address, and every existing-address comparison must stop using the current defaults."}}
